title: Rewinding the whole game
intro: A rewind now puts the whole game back, not just its scene: timers, coroutines, tweens, a script's own fields and the game's random numbers, so it plays on just as it did.
project: Arcade | Whole Rewind
scene: Game

chapter: A script that remembers | A count in a private field, on a timer
write: Scripts/Ticker.cs | rewind-the-whole-game/Ticker.cs
create: Text
rename: Ticker
add: Ticker
add: ScreenAnchor
set: ScreenAnchor.Anchor = Top
set: ScreenAnchor.Offset = 0, -1
code: Assets/Scripts/Ticker.cs | Ticker.cs
say: Here's a little script. Every half second it adds one to a count it keeps in a private field and rolls a die, and the text shows both.
hidecode
play
wait: 3
pause
expectvalue: Ticker.Ticker.InStep = True
explain: game | Playing | The enemies come in waves from a coroutine, each popping in with a tween, and the count goes up on its timer.

chapter: Back in time | The same game goes back
explain: rewind | The Rewind bar | Until now a rewind put back only what the scene file holds. The script started over, its private fields empty, its timer and coroutines gone.
rewind: 0.4
expectvalue: Ticker.Ticker.InStep = True
explain: game | All of it | Now the same game goes back: the count in the private field, the timer partway to its next tick, the waves' coroutine, the tweens, the clock and the random numbers.
pause
wait: 1.5
expectvalue: Ticker.Ticker.InStep = True
explain: game | And on again | It plays on from there just as it did the first time: the same ticks, the same rolls, the same enemies in the same places.
stop
say: Every system keeps its state in its own fields, and a rewind keeps all of them. A test rewinds every template partway and plays it on to the same last bit as the first time through.
